## Compiler ownership

`docs/compiler-design-overview.md` defines the compiler stages, semantic owners and cross-stage handoff for the language surface.

For MON work, read `language-overview/mon-syntax.mtf`, `functions/calls-and-access.mtf`,
`constants/const-records.mtf` and `structs/anonymous-records.mtf` under `docs/docs/`
together with the durable literal-data format authority `docs/docs/mon/mon-format.mtf`
(and teaching `mon-format-basic.mtf`). The shared notation does not merge call-slot validation, folded records
and hidden runtime nominal types into one semantic representation. Moth MON source
syntax allows ordinary expressions according to the receiving context; MON serialised
data is self-contained literal data only, and a MON reader never evaluates Moth
expressions, including foldable ones. The literal-data format and implemented
Rust API/ownership contract are available at `moth_mon`, also deliberately
re-exported as `moth::mon` from `src/lib.rs` with the same API and Rust types.
The standalone owner is `crates/moth-mon/`. The service is executable from
another Rust crate and returns owned values, but compiled Moth programs have no
runtime codec support. Its exact public operations and types are documented in
the standalone crate. `crates/moth-lexical/` owns shared identifier, reserved-word
and numeric-text facts without compiler tokens, type lookup or arithmetic.
Source call-argument and declaration parsers remain compiler-local. The real-source
suite at `src/compiler_tests/mon_syntax_parity/` checks the common notation under
equivalent exact receiving types.
The codec does not imply Moth-native `$mon`, automatic schema extraction or a
static project builder. Source `{=}`, Unicode escapes beyond the existing five
and contextual `::Variant` construction remain named undelivered parity gaps.
